# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: ci-cache-tool-builds — cache the tool builds (clautolisp, alfe, read-autolisp, libredwg) across pipelines
#+STATUS: NEXT
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: ci-cache-tool-builds

* Context

pjb, 2026-08-16: « Un jour on voudrait cacher ces builds. »

Every pipeline rebuilds every tool from scratch, on every runner. Nothing
is reused between pipelines: a one-line documentation change re-links the
SBCL images and recompiles the vendored LibreDWG on each platform that
runs.

The cost is not theoretical. On the =release-1.8.42= tag the Windows job
spent its time compiling LibreDWG, and that build is what exhausted the
machine's memory (=cc1.exe: out of memory=, see
build-tool-paths-unquoted.issue and the =-j2= cap now in
=build-libredwg.sh=). A build that did not need to happen cannot fail.

* Problem

What is rebuilt every time, per platform:

- the SBCL/CCL images for =clautolisp=, =alfe=, =read-autolisp= —
  minutes each, and the dominant cost on the intermittent laptops;
- the vendored *LibreDWG* + the CFFI shim — a full C build of a large
  third-party library, whose inputs change only when the submodule
  pointer or the compiler does;
- the Quicklisp dependency tree, re-resolved per job.

Two of those have inputs that change RARELY and are cheap to name
exactly, which is what makes caching tractable rather than a gamble.

* Proposal

Not a single switch — three separable pieces, in increasing risk:

** 1. LibreDWG + shim (the clear win)

Key the cache on what actually determines the output:

  - the libredwg submodule commit,
  - the compiler identity (=$CC -dumpmachine= + version),
  - =<os>/<arch>=,
  - the build flags in =build-libredwg.sh=.

Any of those changing must miss the cache. =build-libredwg.sh= already
detects a compiler change and resets the CMake state, so the failure mode
of a wrong key is a rebuild, not a corrupt artefact.

** 2. Quicklisp / ASDF fasl cache

=XDG_CACHE_HOME= already points at a per-build cache directory. Keying it
on the Lisp implementation + version + the =.asd= files is standard, and
a stale fasl surfaces as a compile error rather than a silent wrong
binary.

** 3. The tool images themselves (the one to be careful with)

Caching a saved SBCL image means a pipeline can SHIP something it did not
build. A stale image here does not fail loudly — it produces a release
that claims a version it does not contain.

So: *the release jobs must never consume a build cache*, or the key must
include the exact source tree hash of everything linked into the image.
The =check-user-visible-documentation= / =check-release-artefact-set=
checks would not catch a stale image; nothing would.

* Notes

- On SHELL runners (macOS, Windows) the GitLab cache is stored on the
  runner's own disk, so it costs no network. That matters here: uploading
  13 MB from the Windows box fails reproducibly (a middlebox cutting long
  transfers, see the artefact removal in build:alfe:windows), so a cache
  is strictly better than an artefact on that machine.
- On the DOCKER runner each job starts from a fresh container, so the
  cache is the only mechanism available at all.
- Caching interacts with =GIT_CLEAN_FLAGS: -ffd= on Windows (build output
  now survives between jobs there). Decide deliberately whether the cache
  or the surviving working tree is the source of reuse — having both,
  unstated, is how a stale build gets shipped.

* Acceptance criteria

1. A pipeline that changes only documentation reuses the previous
   LibreDWG build on every platform, and says so in the log.
2. Changing the libredwg submodule pointer, or the compiler, misses the
   cache — demonstrated, not assumed.
3. No =release:*= job produces a binary from a cache whose key does not
   pin every source input, and the choice is written down where the next
   reader meets it.
4. Measured before/after on at least the Windows lane, since that is the
   machine the cost falls on.

* Notes from a re-read, 2026-09-28 (nothing implemented)

- *A premise has moved.* "Uploading 13 MB from the Windows box fails
  reproducibly" is no longer true: =release:windows:x86-64= uploaded two zips,
  54 MB together, first time on 2026-09-28 (job 16774988540,
  windows-artifact-upload-fails-on-release). That failure was the 362-entry
  tree, not the size. So on Windows an artefact is an option again, and the
  cache-versus-artefact argument above should be re-weighed.
- *Piece 1 exists, on two lanes only, and may not be doing anything.*
  =release:linux:arm64= and =release:linux:arm32= cache
  =clautolisp/third-party/libredwg/build/= (keys =libredwg-arm64-v1= /
  =-arm32-v1=). Two things to check before building on it:
  1. The key hashes =.gitmodules=, which does NOT change when the libredwg
     submodule moves to another commit (that pointer is in the tree). Not
     dangerous -- the cache is a CMake build directory, so changed sources
     are recompiled -- but it means the key does not express what it looks
     like it expresses (criterion 2).
  2. Those lanes run in a fresh Docker container from a fresh clone, so
     every source file's mtime is the checkout time, NEWER than every cached
     object: make may rebuild everything and the cache save nothing.
     Measure (a warm run's LibreDWG step against a cold one) before
     extending the pattern to other lanes.

* Progress 2026-10-06 (branch ci-cache-tool-builds) -- piece 2 DONE, piece 1 BLOCKED, release lanes cleaned

All measurements below were taken on poseidon in the real CI image
(registry.gitlab.com/ogamita/clautolisp/clautolisp-ci:latest, SBCL 2.2.9 /
CCL 1.13), running the jobs' own script lines in a fresh clone at
/builds/ogamita/clautolisp, with the runner's cache behaviour reproduced
(restored files keep the times they were saved with). No pipeline could be
run: the workflow rules give a plain branch no pipeline, and no MR was to
be opened.

** The trap, measured

Both answers to the note above are now measured, not argued:

- the arm64 release cache saves NOTHING. release-2.1.0 (job 16331860975)
  and release-2.2.1 (job 16386957160) both logged "Successfully extracted
  cache" for libredwg-arm64-v1 and then recompiled all 27 C objects, 29
  minutes each. Reproduced in the image (179 s, 27/27 objects).
- the REVERSE case ships stale code. Fasls of an unchanged tree, made
  newer than a checkout whose alfe version.lisp had changed: alfe-sbcl
  built in 6 s and reported the OLD version. With the saved times instead,
  everything recompiled (146 s): no gain at all.

So file times are never trusted: scripts/ci-build-cache.sh reuses a build
only when a KEY file stored with it equals a content hash of the inputs
(git object ids of every top-level tree entry except issues/,
documentation/, probe-results/, .github/ and the root prose files; the
Lisps' versions; the Quicklisp dist + release directories; the image's
dpkg list), touches the products so the build tool accepts them, and
otherwise discards the cache WHOLE. No per-file reuse: ASDF's dependency
graph does not see compile-time reads (#. of *compile-file-truename*,
empty.dwg embedded by empty-drawing.lisp, cadtui's locale files).

** (2) ASDF fasls -- DONE (Linux docker lanes + macOS unit lanes)

.fasl-cache (.gitlab-ci.yml) on the ten Lisp lanes: build:alfe:sbcl,
test:clautolisp:{sbcl,ccl}, test:clautolisp:compiler:{sbcl,ccl},
test:autolisp-test:sbcl, test:alfe:{sbcl,ccl,conformance,spec-coverage}.
The same template in .gitlab/native.yml on test:{clautolisp,autolisp-test,
alfe}:macos (the macOS runner cleans -ffdx, so it compiles cold every job
today). GitLab key fasl-v1-$CI_JOB_NAME_SLUG: one bounded entry per job;
the KEY file, not the GitLab key, decides validity. The log says
"ci-build-cache[fasl]: HIT ..." or "MISS -- <reason>" (criterion 1, for
the fasls).

Measured, cold -> hit, each job's script (ten at once, as on poseidon):
compiler:sbcl 240->50 s, autolisp-test 219->34, alfe:sbcl 208->25,
clautolisp:sbcl 202->20, alfe:conformance 184->5, build:alfe 182->9,
spec-coverage 178->3, clautolisp:ccl 57->43, alfe:ccl 45->36,
compiler:ccl 29->14. Uncontended build:alfe:sbcl: 131 s -> 5 s. A changed
alfe version.lisp: MISS, 124 s, binary reports the new version. All ten
jobs leave the checkout clean, so all ten save.

A hit only happens when nothing hashed changed (issues/docs/probe-results
pipelines, re-runs); any Lisp change is a full cold compile, exactly as
today. That is the price of never reusing a fasl on faith.

** (1) LibreDWG -- BLOCKED on libredwg-version-is-superproject-describe

The libredwg kind of the script works as a key: same inputs HIT; CC=gcc
instead of cc MISS; the submodule moved to its parent commit MISS
(criterion 2, demonstrated in the image). But a HIT still recompiled 27/27
objects (197 s vs 187 s cold): LibreDWG's CMakeLists runs `git describe' in
cmake's working directory, which is the clautolisp checkout, so config.h --
included by every object -- carries clautolisp's commit id and changes on
every commit. New ticket libredwg-version-is-superproject-describe (it also
means the shipped libredwg reports clautolisp's describe as its version).
Kind left UNWIRED until that is fixed. Outside release lanes only the
manual verify:packaged-dwg:{linux,windows} build LibreDWG anyway.

** (3) Tool images -- not cached, by decision

Images are always dumped by the job from fasls that are either compiled
now or proven to come from the identical tree; caching the image itself
would add nothing safe (the dump is seconds once fasls are warm).

** Release lanes (criterion 3) -- DONE

release:linux:arm64 / :arm32 lost their libredwg cache (cache: []): it did
not pin the submodule commit and saved nothing (above). No release:* job
uses a build cache; the rule is in the .fasl-cache comment, the script
header and AGENTS.md "CI -- build caches". NOT touched, flagged for a
decision: .release-common still carries the autolisp-spec/build/
paged-split cache into release:documentation (keyed on the last commits
of two files, reused by make on times) -- a documentation artefact, not
one of this ticket's tools, but the same question applies.

** Windows -- deliberately unchanged; criterion 4 open

The Windows runner cleans -ffd, so its working tree (clautolisp/.cache,
libredwg/build) survives between jobs: that is the ONE reuse path there,
written down in native.yml and AGENTS.md. No GitLab cache added beside it.
Its reuse remains ASDF's/make's time-based judgement for the non-release
lanes (release-windows.sh clears it); guarding it with the same KEY check
would turn every Lisp change into a ~20 min cold compile there, which is
pjb's call. Criterion 4 (measured before/after on Windows) is NOT met.

** Remaining
- Windows: decide whether to KEY-guard the surviving tree; measure.
- LibreDWG: fix libredwg-version-is-superproject-describe, wire the kind.
- Watch the first master pipelines after merge for HIT/MISS lines and the
  runner's local cache sharing (two poseidon runner registrations, each
  with its own local cache, so hits are per registration).

* Progress 2026-10-07 (clautolisp 2.2.230) -- piece 1 (LibreDWG) UNBLOCKED and wired on Linux

libredwg-version-is-superproject-describe is closed: LibreDWG now carries
its own version (0.14, from clautolisp/third-party/libredwg.release), so
config.h no longer changes with the clautolisp commit. The libredwg kind of
scripts/ci-build-cache.sh is wired on verify:packaged-dwg:linux, the only
non-release Linux lane that builds LibreDWG (GIT_SUBMODULE_STRATEGY
recursive added: the restore runs before `make submodules', and a missing
submodule is a miss that is never saved). Measured in the CI image, two
fresh clones at two clautolisp commits, cache restored with its saved
times: make build-libraries 164 s / 27 objects cold, 1 s / 0 objects on the
HIT; the whole job (build-programs, verify-packaged-dwg.sh) left the
checkout clean and saved both times (~60 MB, 468 files).

Criterion 1 is met for that lane only; release:* lanes stay cold by policy;
Windows is unchanged (its surviving -ffd tree), so criterion 4 stays open --
though that surviving tree should now also stop recompiling LibreDWG on
every clautolisp commit, for the same reason (not measured there).
